For all designs I ask you to make, have them be beautiful, not cookie cutter. Make webpages that are fully featured and worthy for production.

By default, this template supports JSX syntax with Tailwind CSS classes, React hooks, and Lucide React for icons. Do not install other packages for UI themes, icons, etc unless absolutely necessary or I request them.

Use icons from `lucide-react` throughout, **except the four SVGs already provided in `public/assets/`**, which are final art — never generate, redraw or substitute them:
- `logo.svg` — the app logo
- `placeholder-blade.svg`, `placeholder-ratchet.svg`, `placeholder-bit.svg` — shown for any part with no photo, chosen by part class. They are drawn with `currentColor`, so set the colour on the parent element and the silhouette inherits it. Do not hard-code a fill.

Import project modules with the `@/` path alias, which maps to `src/` (e.g. `@/components/Foo` == `src/components/Foo`), instead of deep relative paths like `../../components/Foo`.

Beybox is a Beyblade X collection tracker and WBO-legal deck builder. Vite + React + TypeScript + Tailwind on Bolt Database.

Scope: BX and UX parts only — three-part Beyblades (Blade + Ratchet + Bit). The CX custom line is out of scope.

Users track the parts they own, assemble them into Beys, group three Beys into a deck, and get recommendations. Part data comes from a file I provide and must never be generated, inferred or corrected.

Two field names matter everywhere: show **`display_name`** to users, never `name` — `name` is squashed uppercase like `IMPACTDRAKE`. And the assembly stack uses **`height_mm`** (real millimetres), never `hgt_stat` (a 0–100 rating that happens to be ten times it).

Deck rules are advisory. The app reports whether a deck is tournament-legal; it never prevents one being built or saved.

There are two tiers. Free users get the full catalogue, unlimited inventory, three decks, and scored suggestions from parts they already own. Beybox Pro is a one-time unlock that adds unlimited decks.

**The app uses no file storage anywhere.** Every image is a static file already in `public/`. Never add an upload control, a file input, a storage bucket or an image URL field — not on any form, including the ones that create parts. A part with no photo shows the placeholder SVG for its class.

I prefer simple, direct implementations over clever or highly abstracted ones.